      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In darker skin tones, erythema is less visible and often requires distinguishing it from surrounding macular brown pigmentation by observing whether the redness has lighter or clearer borders. Instead of bright redness, inflammation may appear as purple, blue, gray, or brown hues, which are primarily visible under magnification. Therefore, a standard approach involves using white light, contrast imaging, or dermoscopy to visualize the bowel wall structure rather than relying solely on naked-eye color changes.",
